{
  "requirement": "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references` has two obligations. A bundled skill MAY link to files for specialized material, which imposes nothing to check. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. The second is one each skill site upholds on its own, so it is reviewed per site. Nothing in the text needs separate code, so no obligation falls between the sites and the tests.\n\n**Site query `skills`.** I ran `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references/skills`. It matched exactly five files: the `SKILL.md` of `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`. A search for `name: habeas` across the repository found the same five front-matter blocks and no bundled skill elsewhere. The `references/*.md` files carry no front matter, so the query correctly leaves them out. The query neither misses a skill nor selects anything outside the requirement's scope.\n\n**Query snippets.** The `match` snippet has front matter followed by a heading. The `no_match` snippet is the same heading without front matter. Together they show that the query selects on the `minus_metadata` node, which is what \"recognized by its front matter\" means. This is adequate.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_say_when_to_read_each_reference_file]` is a supplementary check on the sites.\n- It lists the 7 same-skill links that exist: `habeas` to `ci.md`, `distributed-requirements.md` and `syntax-details.md`; `habeas-requirements` to `review-prompt.md`; and `habeas-evidence` to `authoring-failures.md`, `fixme.md` and `reviews.md`.\n- Each entry carries the exact condition-bearing sentence, for example \"Use the CI example … when adding these steps\", \"If authoring fails on …\" or \"a FIXME judgment … read …\".\n- For every skill in `SKILLS` and every file under its `references/` directory, via `skill_files(name)[1..]`, it asserts that at least one such expectation exists. It then asserts that the collapsed `SKILL.md` text contains each expected sentence. A new reference file with no expectation, or a changed or removed \"when to read\" sentence, fails the test.\n\nCross-skill links, such as `../habeas/references/ci.md` in the bootstrap and evidence skills, are not covered by this test. Each site's own review covers them, so they are not a gap here.\n\nNo acknowledgments are needed and I found no gaps.",
  "references": "5b5a1f7cf171bc78f06adc8576f6e3e3dcb20c5f062af5c778e2b34a76dbfa84",
  "inputs": [
